NAME
       head - print the first lines of files

SYNOPSIS
       head [OPTION]... [FILE]...

DESCRIPTION
       head prints the first 10 lines of each FILE (or of standard input when
       there is no FILE or FILE is -). With more than one file, each part is
       preceded by a header line "==> FILE <==". It is handy for a quick look
       at the start of a log or a big output: ps aux --sort=-%cpu | head.

OPTIONS
       -n, --lines=N
              Print the first N lines instead of 10. With -n -N, print every
              line except the last N. The old form -N (head -5) also works.

       -c, --bytes=N
              Print the first N bytes.

       -q, --quiet, --silent
              Never print the headers.

       -v, --verbose
              Always print the headers.

EXAMPLES
              $ head -n 3 /etc/passwd
              $ ps aux --sort=-%mem | head -5
              $ head -n 20 /var/log/messages | tail -n 5   (lines 16 to 20)

SEE ALSO
       tail(1), less(1), sed(1)
